# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# EGO-Lines Automotive — environment variables
#
# LOCAL DEV: copy this file to .env.local and fill in real values.
#            .env.local is gitignored and must never be committed.
#
# GITHUB CODESPACES: the secret-bearing variables below are injected
#            directly into the environment via repository Codespaces
#            secrets (Settings → Secrets and variables → Codespaces)
#            rather than written to a file on disk. prisma.config.ts
#            calls dotenv's config({ path: '.env.local' }), which is a
#            silent no-op when that file is absent — so the same config
#            file works unchanged in both setups.
#
# Never commit real values for anything in this file.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── Database (Supabase Postgres) ──
# DATABASE_URL: transaction pooler, port 6543, used by the running app
#               via @prisma/adapter-pg. Append ?pgbouncer=true.
# DIRECT_URL:   session/direct connection, port 5432, used by the Prisma
#               CLI for migrations. DDL is unreliable through the
#               transaction pooler, so these must stay separate.
DATABASE_URL=
DIRECT_URL=

# ── Supabase (Auth + Storage) ──
# NOTE: these use Supabase's current key naming (publishable / secret).
# The older anon / service_role names are deprecated — the application
# code reads the names below, so keep them in sync.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY is inlined into the client bundle
# and is safe to expose. SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY bypasses all row-level
# security and must NEVER be exposed to the browser or prefixed
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ — server-side use only.
#
# Storage: vehicle photographs live in a `vehicle-photos` bucket, created by
# `npm run storage:setup`. It is public for READS only — the photographs are
# marketing material shown to anonymous visitors — and carries no write policy
# for any browser session, so uploads are only possible through a Server
# Action holding SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY. Payment receipts (Stage 22) must get a
# separate PRIVATE bucket read through signed URLs; do not reuse this one.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=
SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=

# ── Public site configuration ──
# Neither of these is a secret. Absent values degrade gracefully:
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL falls back to the production domain, and an unset
# WhatsApp number causes every WhatsApp CTA to render nothing rather
# than link to a broken wa.me URL (see src/lib/utils/whatsapp.ts).
#
# ⚠️ NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL is REQUIRED in production. It is the origin used
# to build the links inside Supabase authentication emails (invitations and
# password resets). Deriving that origin from the request's Host header
# instead would allow password-reset poisoning — a forged Host produces a
# genuine reset email whose link points at an attacker's domain, carrying a
# live recovery token. src/lib/actions/auth.actions.ts therefore refuses to
# fall back to headers when NODE_ENV=production; in development it does,
# so the flow works on localhost and in Codespaces.
#
# Whatever value is used here must also be listed under
# Supabase → Authentication → URL Configuration → Redirect URLs.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_WHATSAPP_NUMBER: a FALLBACK only, in full international format
# (e.g. +211900000000).
#
# The number the site actually uses is the one an administrator sets in the
# dashboard under Settings, stored in BusinessSettings.whatsappNumber — the
# brief requires it to be configurable without a deploy. This variable is
# used only while that field is still blank, which is the window between a
# first deploy and someone opening Settings.
#
# If both are empty, every WhatsApp call to action renders nothing at all.
# That is deliberate: a link to a wrong number is worse than no link.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_WHATSAPP_NUMBER=

# ── Search engine verification (optional) ──
# Ownership tokens for Google Search Console and Bing Webmaster Tools, by the
# "HTML tag" method: paste only the content="…" value, not the whole tag. They
# are published in every page's <head>, so they are not secrets. Leave empty
# when the domain is verified by DNS instead (the recommended method — it
# covers http/https and www/non-www in one Domain property).
GOOGLE_SITE_VERIFICATION=
BING_SITE_VERIFICATION=

# ── Email (Resend) ──
# Real, server-side sending through Resend (https://resend.com — free
# signup, no card required). One key powers every email the site sends:
#   - "Send quotation" → Email / Both, in the admin quote screen
#   - automatic customer emails: request received, order confirmed, payment
#     receipt, refund, tracking number, and every tracking update
#   - the new-quote-request alert to every active administrator
# WhatsApp click-to-chat never touches this.
#
# RESEND_API_KEY: from the Resend dashboard → API Keys. Server-side only,
# never exposed to the browser. Leave blank during setup — nothing fails:
# the dispatch dialog says email isn't configured, admin actions say the
# customer was not emailed, and a warning is logged on the server.
#
# EMAIL_FROM_ADDRESS: the verified sender identity, e.g.
# "EGO-Lines Automotive <quotes@your-domain.com>". Optional while this
# project has no domain yet — omitted, it falls back to Resend's shared
# sandbox sender (onboarding@resend.dev), which needs no domain but is
# typically restricted to sending to the account's own signed-up address
# until a domain is verified. Once a domain is verified in the Resend
# dashboard, set this — no code change needed.
RESEND_API_KEY=
EMAIL_FROM_ADDRESS=

# ── End-to-end testing (optional) ──
# Email of an existing administrator for Playwright to sign in as. When set
# — alongside SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY — the authenticated admin test projects are
# included in the run; without it they are omitted entirely rather than
# skipped, so "the admin tests did not run" is obvious rather than buried.
#
# Deliberately has no default: the suite writes to the real BusinessSettings
# row (restoring what it found), and defaulting this would let it act on
# whichever administrator happened to exist in whatever environment it was
# pointed at.
E2E_ADMIN_EMAIL=

# ── Web Push: notifications to the admin dashboard app ──
# One key pair per deployment. Generate it once with:
#     npx web-push generate-vapid-keys
# and keep it: replacing the pair silently stops notifications on every
# device until each administrator switches them on again in
# Settings → Notifications.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY is public by design (browsers need it to
# subscribe). VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY is a secret — server-side only, never
# NEXT_PUBLIC_. VAPID_SUBJECT is how push services contact the operator
# about abuse: "mailto:you@your-domain.com" or an https:// URL. Left blank it
# falls back to the site URL (when https) or the business email in Settings.
#
# Without the two keys the dashboard still works; the notification controls
# say push is not set up on the server.
NEXT_PUBLIC_VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
VAPID_SUBJECT=

# ── Monitoring: Sentry error reporting ──
# All optional — without them Sentry is simply off and nothing is sent.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN: Sentry → Project → Settings → Client Keys (DSN).
#   Not a secret (it only lets the site send events), so NEXT_PUBLIC_ is right.
#   Error reports are privacy-scrubbed before sending (src/lib/monitoring/scrub.ts).
#   It is compiled into the browser code, so on Vercel it must be set BEFORE the
#   deployment is built — adding it later needs a redeploy. Prove the key with
#   `npm run sentry:test`, which sends one test event and reports the answer.
#   (A server-only SENTRY_DSN is also read, but it cannot reach the browser —
#   use NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN.)
# SENTRY_ORG / SENTRY_PROJECT: the organisation and project slugs from the
#   Sentry URL, e.g. https://<org>.sentry.io/projects/<project>/
# SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN: Sentry → Settings → Auth Tokens (Organization Token).
#   A SECRET. Used only at build time to upload source maps so stack traces
#   are readable; the maps are then deleted from the public build. Without
#   it, errors are still reported, just with minified stack traces.
#
# Verify the LIVE deployment (not just the key) by opening, while signed in
# to the dashboard as an administrator:
#   /api/monitoring/verify                  → JSON: enabled / delivered
#   /api/monitoring/verify?mode=unhandled   → a real uncaught server error
NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN=
SENTRY_ORG=
SENTRY_PROJECT=
SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN=
